Thanks for all the discussion. Honestly, it is difficult to convince anyone without rather rigorous and complete chains of theoretical and experimental evidence. I realized that several years ago. So my students and I had been working intensively to put together a complete framework. We are finishing a manuscript which we will share publicly soon. The connection between the classic analytical approach and the modern non parametric approach will be evident, among many other seemingly separate techniques and approaches developed under different names and at different times. All Roads Lead to Rome! By Rome, I mean parsimonious representation of data distribution.
Quoting @yuxiangw_cs
@matloff @YiMaTweets Can’t speak for @YiMaTweets but a “general natural law” seems too strong. You can certainly find data / distributions that are above Zipf law. Those are however much harder to learn (unlearnable at d —&gt; inf limit), while those below Zipf are easy to learn.

Any scientists should have a fundamental drive to understand and explain about anything that is significant in nature. Historically, blackboxes always brew superstitions, fears and worships, and lead to suppression, manipulation, and exploitation. I believe that is happening to AI too.
Quoting @GaryMarcus
@zjasper @YiMaTweets @ylecun lecun has hardly favored white box?

The trajectory of a linear system is not necessarily linear. However the distribution of the trajectories is… this is the basis for linear system identification… please check the introduction of my previous book!
Quoting @mengyer
@YiMaTweets A linear system is not necessarily straight. In a discrete linear system, x_t+1= A x_t + B u _t, straightness can be thought of as the closeness of A (or state transition Jacobian in nonlinear case) from identity (1 minus spectral norm of A-I).

It is ok for an individual researcher not to know everything. But systematic ignorance by an almost entire community is never acceptable!
Quoting @yacineMTB
@slwlyfade2blk @YiMaTweets It's hard to know everything!

clean_summary:
Science is never based on anyone’s opinions. It should be based on rigorous evidence, experimental or theoretical.

@Kekius_Sage: "Elon Musk just settled the biggest AGI debate in tech. Yann LeCun said: 'Even humans don't have general intelligence. We evolved to specialize, not to be universal.' Demis Hassabis said: 'You're confusing AGI with universal intelligence. The human brain is a biological version of the Turing machine, and AI can absolutely reach that level of generality.' Elon Musk affirmed: 'Demis is right.'"

The debate centers on whether AI can achieve human-like general intelligence, with one side arguing humans are not necessarily that smart and the other emphasizing AI's potential to reach that level.

Interestingly, the last chapter of my An Invitation to 3D Vision book explains why symmetry makes 3D perception easier. Seeing abstract patterns in data appears to be a human's unique ability. This ability to abstract patterns might stem from an extreme form of compression, or perhaps something more than that.

@pmddomingos: "Why do humans generalize so much better than deep networks? Because they learn something deep networks can't: symmetries."

The linked arXiv paper discusses how deep networks can learn symmetries from data through a neural kernel theory, shedding light on the differences in generalization capabilities.
